A code smell is a surface symptom in code that usually points to a deeper design problem (Fowler, Refactoring). The program still works; the smell makes it harder to change.

1. Feature Envy — a method uses another class's data more than its own.
class Invoice {
  double discount(Customer c) {               // reads only Customer fields
    if (c.getYears() > 5 && c.getTotalBought() > 100000) return 0.10;
    return c.isCorporate() ? 0.05 : 0.0;
  }
}
Fix: Move Method → Customer.discountRate(); Invoice calls customer.discountRate().

2. Dead Code — code that can never run or whose result is never used.
int fee(int amount) {
  int old = amount * 2;            // value never used
  return amount / 100;
  // log("fee computed");          // unreachable after return
}
plus a method calcFeeV1() that nothing calls. Fix: delete it (version control keeps history); compilers and IDE warnings help find it.

3. Duplicate Code — the same logic in more than one place.
double vatOnSale(double x)   { return Math.round(x * 0.15 * 100) / 100.0; }
double vatOnService(double x){ return Math.round(x * 0.15 * 100) / 100.0; }
Fix: Extract Method → vat(double x) in one place (or Pull Up Method into a common superclass); a rate change then touches one line.

4. Shotgun Surgery — one change forces many small edits across many classes.
Adding a new currency requires edits in Invoice, Receipt, Report, Ledger, PdfExporter and ApiMapper because each formats money itself.
Fix: Move Method/Move Field so all money handling lives in one Money class; the change is made once.

| Smell | Group (Refactoring.Guru) | Main refactoring |
|---|---|---|
| Feature Envy | couplers | Move Method |
| Dead Code | dispensables | Remove / delete |
| Duplicate Code | dispensables | Extract Method, Pull Up Method |
| Shotgun Surgery | change preventers | Move Method, Move Field, Inline Class |

