A design pattern is a named, proven, reusable solution to a problem that recurs in software design within a given context. It is a template, not finished code. The standard catalogue is "Design Patterns: Elements of Reusable Object-Oriented Software" (Gamma, Helm, Johnson, Vlissides — the Gang of Four, 1994): 23 patterns.

| Category | Purpose | Examples (count) |
|---|---|---|
| Creational | how objects are created | Singleton, Factory Method, Abstract Factory, Builder, Prototype (5) |
| Structural | how classes/objects are composed | Adapter, Bridge, Composite, Decorator, Facade, Flyweight, Proxy (7) |
| Behavioural | how objects communicate and share responsibility | Observer, Strategy, Command, State, Iterator, Template Method, Chain of Responsibility, Mediator, Memento, Visitor, Interpreter (11) |

Five with categories: Singleton (creational), Factory Method (creational), Adapter (structural), Observer (behavioural), Strategy (behavioural). Architectural patterns such as MVC, layered and microservices are a separate, higher level.

Singleton
- Intent: ensure a class has exactly one instance and give a global access point to it.
- Use: configuration manager, logger, connection-pool manager, a bank's single audit-log writer.
- Structure (UML): one class box Singleton with -instance: Singleton {static, underlined}, -Singleton() private constructor, +getInstance(): Singleton {static}.
- Code sketch:
public final class Logger {
  private static volatile Logger instance;
  private Logger() {}
  public static Logger getInstance() {
    if (instance == null) {
      synchronized (Logger.class) {
        if (instance == null) instance = new Logger();
      }
    }
    return instance;
  }
}
- Benefits: controlled single instance, lazy creation, saves resources.
- Drawbacks: acts as hidden global state, harder to unit-test, needs care under multithreading (the double-checked lock with volatile above).

Benefits of patterns in general: proven solutions, shared vocabulary in a team, more flexible and maintainable designs.

