public class ConfigDemo {
    static final class AppConfig {
        private static volatile AppConfig instance;     // one shared object
        private int created = 0;
        private final String dbUrl;
        private AppConfig() { dbUrl = "jdbc:postgresql://db/cbs"; }   // private: no 'new' outside
        static AppConfig getInstance() {
            if (instance == null) {                          // first check, no lock
                synchronized (AppConfig.class) {
                    if (instance == null) {                  // second check, with lock
                        instance = new AppConfig();
                        instance.created++;
                    }
                }
            }
            return instance;
        }
        String dbUrl() { return dbUrl; }
        int created() { return created; }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        AppConfig[] seen = new AppConfig[4];
        Thread[] t = new Thread[4];
        for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
            final int k = i;
            t[i] = new Thread(() -> seen[k] = AppConfig.getInstance());
            t[i].start();
        }
        for (Thread th : t) th.join();
        boolean same = true;
        for (AppConfig c : seen) same &= (c == seen[0]);
        System.out.println("same object in all threads: " + same);
        System.out.println("constructor ran " + AppConfig.getInstance().created() + " time(s)");
        System.out.println("db = " + AppConfig.getInstance().dbUrl());
    }
}

Output (compiled and run with javac/java 21):
same object in all threads: true
constructor ran 1 time(s)
db = jdbc:postgresql://db/cbs

Key points: the private constructor blocks new AppConfig() outside the class; the static volatile field holds the only instance; double-checked locking creates it once even when threads race, and volatile stops another thread from seeing a half-built object. Simpler alternatives: an eager static final instance, or an enum singleton.

